[For "white"]
[Kind "gambit"]
[Start "qg-accepted-main 2.c4"]
[Name "The Albin Countergambit: 2...e5"]

2... e5 { The Albin Countergambit. Black gives a pawn to drive a wedge into White's position. [%cal Re5d4] }
3. dxe5 { Takes it. Black's idea only works if White goes wrong next move. }
3... d4 { The wedge. It cramps White and takes c3 and e3 away from the pieces. [%csl Yd4] }
4. Nf3 { Develops, guards e5 and keeps the position closed. }
( 4. e3? { Looks natural, because it hits the wedge at once. But the wedge can take on e3 at the right moment, and that is the Lasker Trap. [%tag trap] }
  4... Bb4+ 5. Bd2 dxe3 { The pawn now hits both the bishop on d2 and f2. 6.fxe3 is the only safe reply. }
  6. Bxb4 { Takes a bishop, and it looks as if White has won a piece. }
  6... exf2+ 7. Ke2 { Forced, and now 7...fxg1=N+ wins. The pawn becomes a knight, because a knight gives check, and after 8.Rxg1 Bg4+ White's queen is lost. } )
4... Nc6
5. Nbd2 { The knight is heading for b3, where it attacks the wedge. Developing this way also leaves the c1 bishop free. [%cal Gd2b3,Gb3d4] }
5... Bg4 { Pins the f3 knight, which guards e5. }
6. a3 { Takes b4 away from Black's pieces and prepares b4. White has a pawn extra and can afford a move like this. [%cal Ga3b4] }
6... Nge7 { Heading for g6, to win e5 back. }
( 6... Bc5? { Develops, but the bishop is a target for White's queenside pawns. }
  7. b4 { Gains a move on the bishop and takes more room. White keeps the extra pawn, and the engine has White more than three pawns ahead. [%cal Gb4c5] } )
7. h3 { Asks the bishop to decide. }
7... Bxf3
8. Nxf3 { White now has the two bishops. }
8... Ng6
9. g3 { The bishop goes to g2. Black can win the pawn on e5 back, but the f1 bishop then has the whole long diagonal, and the wedge on d4 is weak. White is clearly better. [%cal Gf1g2,Gg2b7] } *
